title: Sync conflicts
tags: Sync
kind: guide
summary: A field, a position, a file's content or its mode changed on both sides: decide by a rule of the intents file, by --on-conflict, or when the plan asks — and change your mind in the plan before the run.

A ''conflict'' is something changed on both sides since the last sync, to
different values ([[Change detection in sync]]): a field, the position
(`mfr_path`), a file's content (`mfr_content`) or its mode (`mfr_permissions`). At
the first sync of a link, two different values are a conflict.

!! Deciding

The plan decides each conflict when it writes it, by the first of:

* `--on-conflict ask|skip|prefer:<repo>` on `mf sync plan`, which overrides everything;
* the first matching `[[conflict]]` rule of the intents file ([[The intents file]]);
* otherwise, ''ask''.

`prefer:<repo>` keeps that repository's value — by name or uuid. `skip` leaves both
sides as they are: the link is not recorded, and the conflict comes back at the next
plan, until it is decided.

Asked, the plan shows the field, the record's path and each repository's value, and
reads one letter: `a` or `b` keeps that repository's value, `l` leaves the whole link
out of this plan, anything else — an empty line, no terminal — skips the field. The
GUI never asks: its conflicts are skipped, to be decided in the panel.

!! Changing a decision

Each conflict is an operation of the plan, its decision in `plan_resolve`
([[The sync plan]]); the run reads it then, so it can still change:

```
mf sync show laptop nas --conflicts
mf -u <plan> metarecord -q 'plan_kind = "conflict"' get --select '*'
mf -u <plan> metarecord -i <op> field set plan_resolve:string=b
```

The [[sync panel]] lists the conflicts with a `skip` / `a` / `b` choice each.
